# Copy to sabha.conf (gitignored) and fill in. $VAR and ~ expand; earlier keys can be used later.
# Quote a path that holds a space. SABHA_WS: where the roles start (the folder that holds your repos).
# SABHA_IMPL: the sabha folder, the team's shared files (status/, inbox/, protocol/); keep it apart from your repos.
SABHA_WS="$HOME/Work"
SABHA_IMPL="$HOME/my-claude-sabha"
# Optional: where FOR-ME.md and FOR-ME.html are written (default: SABHA_IMPL). Keep it outside this repo.
#SABHA_FORME_DIR=$SABHA_WS/path/to/board
#SABHA_NIGHTLY_DIR=$SABHA_WS/path/to/updates   # sabha nightly drafts, one <date>.md per day (default: SABHA_IMPL/nightly)
#SABHA_CSV_DIR=$SABHA_WS/path/to/tracker   # agents.csv and for-me.csv, written on every render
#SABHA_PRUNE_CMD=$SABHA_WS/path/to/wt-inventory.sh   # sabha prune: the inventory script, run with --actions only
#SABHA_PRUNE_DIR=$SABHA_WS/path/to/reports   # sabha prune reports, one <date>.md per day (default: SABHA_IMPL/prune)
# Tracker: html (default: offline page from the CSVs, no login, tracker/build-html.py), xlsx (tracker/build-xlsx.py, import
# into any spreadsheet) or sheet (Google Sheet: the better option for phone use, sharing and the Inbox/Decisions round
# trip; one-time `bin/sabha-sheet auth`, then `bin/sabha-sheet sync` copies the CSVs, no agent needed).
#SABHA_TRACKER=html
#SABHA_SHEET_ID=
# 1 = an agent session that has the Google Sheets connector also syncs every 30 min (tracker/cron-prompts.md). Default 0
# on every plan: that is 48 agent runs a day (about 24 per 12 working hours), each a model call that spends
# subscription usage even when nothing changed (token cost per run not measured). `bin/sabha-sheet sync` on a timer
# gives the same result at zero usage; turn this on only if you cannot run the script.
#SABHA_SHEET_AGENT_SYNC=0
# The opening prompt says "You are <Name> in $SABHA_PROJECT session setup"; the relay reports to $SABHA_USER.
SABHA_PROJECT="My project"
SABHA_USER="the user"
# Terminal backend: herdr (default, every OS; install with brew install herdr). iterm = the frozen macOS iTerm2 path.
# On Windows run the scripts in Git Bash (UNTESTED). Nudge a role: bin/sabha-nudge <Name> "text". Mac/Linux: run
# `herdr attach` in a VS Code integrated terminal to view and chat with any pane.
#SABHA_BACKEND=herdr
# herdr session sabha uses (default sabha). Sabha never uses herdr's default session, which holds your own workspaces;
# a named session has no server until sabha-up.sh starts it, and `sabha-down.sh --yes` stops and deletes only this one.
#SABHA_HERDR_SESSION=sabha
# 1 passes --remote-control to Claude panes (Pro/Max only; an org policy can block it).
#SABHA_REMOTE=0
# sabha owe: open for-me items whose title or why holds one of these words; drafts (.md or .txt, no sent: line, no
# <file>.sent marker) in these folders (space-separated, relative = under SABHA_WS); an optional board export (JSON
# list of rows with id, dependsOn, unblocks) names the rows each one blocks.
#SABHA_OWE_WORDS="send message reply post"
# Add words such as "tell" here; "ask <Name>" (capital N) always counts.
#SABHA_OWE_DIRS="docs/drafts"
#SABHA_OWE_BOARD=$SABHA_WS/path/to/board.json
# Plan: the roster, panes, copies and labels of sabha.<plan>.conf beside this file (pro, max5, max20) replace the ones
# below. `sabha-up.sh --plan pro` does the same for one run. Leave it unset to use the roster written here.
#SABHA_PLAN=pro
# Roster (sabha.max20.conf is the reference copy of this default list; tests/test-roster.sh fails when they differ):
# Name:agent:model[:effort[:flags]] entries separated by spaces (a name with a space is not supported; effort
# defaults to medium). The agent is claude, opencode, codex, agy or muse; a row without one (Name:model...) is claude,
# so older rosters still parse. Claude rows start from sabha-up.sh and sabha-run; the other agents start through herdr
# (opencode tested; codex, agy and muse UNTESTED) and sabha-run never serves them. The one roster source
# for the launchers, sabha-watch and the board. Order matters: the relay first, the dispatcher second, then the panes
# in tab order (each tab: top row left to right, then the bottom row, up to 8).
SABHA_ROSTER="Relay:claude:opus:medium Dispatcher:claude:opus:medium Architect:claude:fable:medium BuilderHeavy:claude:opus:high BuilderPrecise:claude:opus:medium Reviewer:claude:sonnet:high Writer:claude:sonnet:medium Runner:claude:sonnet:medium Scribe:claude:haiku:high BackendCritic:claude:opus:high:gate,lazy FrontendCritic:claude:sonnet:high:gate,lazy SecurityCritic:claude:opus:high:gate,lazy ScopeCritic:claude:sonnet:high:gate,lazy BlindReviewer:claude:sonnet:medium:gate,lazy Keeper:claude:sonnet:medium:gate,lazy Observer:claude:haiku:medium:gate Housekeeper:claude:haiku:medium:gate Tutor:claude:opus:medium:ondemand Watcher:claude:haiku:high:ondemand Coach:claude:sonnet:medium:ondemand"
# Flags (comma list): ondemand = no pane, start it with sabha-up.sh <Name>; gate = a pane in the second tab (Gate);
# lazy = no pane; bin/sabha-run starts it headless when a brief lands; mcp = keeps the user's MCP servers. No flag = a
# Build-tab pane.
# Panes: when set, exactly these roles get a pane, in this order (the relay first, so it restarts like the rest);
# every other profile is on demand: a subagent of the dispatcher with its own model and effort, or sabha-up.sh <Name>.
# When unset, the flags above decide.
#SABHA_PANES="Relay Dispatcher BuilderHeavy BuilderPrecise Writer"
# Efficiency (docs/specs/efficiency-profiles.md). 1 (default) = on: a profile from cores and RAM (the lower tier wins)
# caps the panes when SABHA_PANES is unset (lite 2, standard 4, full 7: the first panes in roster order) and sets the
# defaults of SABHA_RUN_MAX and SABHA_HEAVY_MAX (lite 1, standard 2, full 3); --print gives the sabha-loop line; per-role
# MCP sets. Any other value = off, the behaviour before it. SABHA_PROFILE: auto (default), lite, standard or full.
SABHA_EFFICIENCY=1
#SABHA_PROFILE=auto
# Heavy-job gate (bin/sabha-heavy): GO when load1 <= LOAD_FACTOR x cores, memory pressure green, fewer than HEAVY_MAX
# slots held, SETTLE seconds since the last slot. acquire polls up to WAIT seconds; a slot without a heartbeat for TTL
# seconds is stale; FREE_PCT is the memory_pressure -Q fallback (free percent under it = yellow).
#SABHA_HEAVY_MAX=2
#SABHA_HEAVY_LOAD_FACTOR=2
#SABHA_HEAVY_SETTLE=45
#SABHA_HEAVY_WAIT=900
#SABHA_HEAVY_TTL=1800
#SABHA_HEAVY_FREE_PCT=40
# Queue (`sabha-heavy queue add|list|next|remove`): a waiting job gains one priority point per AGE seconds so it cannot
# starve; entries older than QUEUE_TTL seconds are dropped. A job's own settle: `queue add <label> --settle <secs>`
# or SABHA_HEAVY_SETTLE_FOR on acquire/run. Every start, end and give-up goes to status/heavy/jobs.tsv.
#SABHA_HEAVY_AGE=600
#SABHA_HEAVY_QUEUE_TTL=86400
# Starter: with the flag on, `sabha-up.sh --print <Name>` prints the sabha-loop line; --print --raw prints the bare
# claude line. MCP per role: SABHA_MCP_<Name> = full, none, or set names (a,b), each set a JSON file in the shape
# `claude --mcp-config` reads. The relay and the dispatcher default to full, every other role to none (the roster flag
# "mcp" still means full). A brief header "mcp: full" gives that one headless run (bin/sabha-run) the full set.
#SABHA_MCP_BuilderPrecise=aikido
#SABHA_MCP_FILE_aikido=$SABHA_IMPL/mcp/aikido.json
# Copies: BuilderPrecise-1, BuilderPrecise-2 ... take BuilderPrecise's profile with their own inbox, status file and worktree; at most this many
# per role (2 on Pro, 4 on Max).
#SABHA_MAX_COPIES=2
# Role labels, shown as "Architect (Architect)" in the opening prompt and on the board.
#SABHA_LABEL_Architect="Architect"
# A role id is one word (Ved Vyasa is Keeper). SABHA_PROMPT_<Name> replaces the
# opening prompt after "then status/<name>.md, " (no double quotes, $ or backticks after expansion).
# The relay briefs and asks; it never pushes, posts or restarts panes (sabha-loop restarts them, the human pushes).
SABHA_PROMPT_Relay="then inbox/Relay/ (ack any brief without an ack; resume any with an ack and no done), and continue from the status file's top block. You are the relay: turn each request into a brief for the dispatcher, or run the one command the user wrote in this message. Never push, post or publish, never run a queued push, never restart, type into or press keys in a pane: queue the exact command in for-me.items for the user. Write your handoff to status/relay.md when sabha-loop warns (250k tokens); it restarts you fresh at 300k. When SABHA_BOARD_URL is set, keep the board current: write each human decision the same turn, read remote edits at every status check. Report to the user."
SABHA_PROMPT_Tutor="then ask the user which topic to practise. You are the on-demand tutor: one open question at a time, read-only on code, no briefs. Report to the user directly."
# SABHA_READS_<Name> replaces what the role reads first (the protocol core, its role file, its status file): for a role
# that keeps its own role card and memory files. The tail (inbox, or SABHA_PROMPT_<Name>) still follows.
#SABHA_READS_Architect="$SABHA_IMPL/architect/ROLE.md, then every file in $SABHA_IMPL/architect/MEMORY/"
# Headless runner (bin/sabha-run, docs/headless-runner.md). Which roles it serves: lazy (default), all, or names.
#SABHA_RUN_ROLES=lazy
# At most this many runs at once (default 2); a run is stopped after SABHA_RUN_TIMEOUT seconds (default 3600).
#SABHA_RUN_MAX=2
#SABHA_RUN_TIMEOUT=3600
# One log per run (default $SABHA_IMPL/runs). SABHA_RUN_AUTO=1 makes each sabha-watch run end with a sabha-run pass.
#SABHA_RUN_LOGS=$SABHA_IMPL/runs
#SABHA_RUN_AUTO=1
# 1 = the relay may only read, brief (inbox/, memory) and ask; bin/sabha-relay-guard denies the rest. "you do it" as the
# first line of a prompt = one turn. On here; sabha-up.sh defaults to 0 when the conf does not set it. If the guard
# locks you out (a broken conf denies every call), set 0 here and restart the relay.
SABHA_RELAY_GUARD=1
# More files the guarded relay may write besides inbox/, its status/<name>.md, for-me.items and memory: absolute
# paths, space-separated, a glob in the last part only, one directory deep.
#SABHA_RELAY_WRITE="$SABHA_WS/docs/reviews/*-review.md $SABHA_WS/docs/questions/*"
# The relay's sabha-loop warns (log and a nudge to write the handoff) at SABHA_RELAY_WARN_K thousand tokens and restarts
# it fresh at SABHA_RELAY_STOP_K once the handoff is saved; SABHA_RELAY_WINDOW_K is its context window size. For the
# relay these replace SABHA_LOOP_LIMIT, even one you set.
#SABHA_RELAY_WARN_K=250
#SABHA_RELAY_STOP_K=300
#SABHA_RELAY_WINDOW_K=1000
# Starts per brief (default 1 = no retry). Only a CLI or machine failure is retried, after SABHA_RUN_BACKOFF seconds
# doubled per attempt; a timeout or the agent's own BLOCKED done is final (docs/headless-runner.md).
#SABHA_RUN_ATTEMPTS=1
#SABHA_RUN_BACKOFF=60
# Pane supervisor (bin/sabha-loop <Name>) and usage alerts (bin/statusline-sabha.sh, bin/sabha-limits.sh).
# Claude panes restart fresh at SABHA_LOOP_LIMIT percent context once the handoff is saved; a context file older
# than SABHA_LOOP_STALE seconds only warns. SABHA_NOTIFY lists the usage percentages (5-hour and 7-day windows) that
# fire a notification, each once per window. At SABHA_STOP percent no new session starts until the window resets
# (0 or off disables). SABHA_PUSH=ntfy also sends each alert to the ntfy topic (public topics are readable by name).
#SABHA_LOOP_LIMIT=30
#SABHA_LOOP_STALE=120
#SABHA_NOTIFY="10 20 30 40 50 60 70 80 90"
#SABHA_STOP=90
#SABHA_PUSH=off
#SABHA_NTFY_TOPIC=
#SABHA_NTFY_URL=https://ntfy.sh
# Accounts: run roles on different Claude accounts (README, "Running roles on different Claude accounts"). Name the
# accounts; get each token with `claude setup-token`; keep it as CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN_ACCOUNT_<NAME> in the environment
# or in SABHA_ENV_FILE (default $SABHA_WS/.env; empty = environment only; never commit it). SABHA_ACCOUNT_<RoleId> puts a
# role on an account; a role without one uses the default login. A named account with no token refuses to start (the
# message names the variable); `bin/sabha-check` lists every role's account. SABHA_STOP_<NAME> is that account's own stop
# line (limits.txt is shared per kit; without it, SABHA_STOP applies to every account).
#SABHA_ACCOUNTS="HV AU"
#SABHA_ACCOUNT_BuilderHeavy=AU
#SABHA_STOP_AU=95
#SABHA_ENV_FILE=$SABHA_WS/.env
# Keep the Mac awake while sabha runs (docs/keep-awake.md). auto (default): AC power -> lid, battery -> open, switched
# live when the power source changes. open = no idle sleep while the herdr server lives, a closed lid still sleeps;
# lid = open plus a closed lid keeps running (needs a sudo step you run); off = nothing. The pair below overrides
# what auto picks per power source. `sabha keep-awake <mode>` rewrites SABHA_KEEP_AWAKE.
#SABHA_KEEP_AWAKE=auto
#SABHA_KEEP_AWAKE_AC=lid
#SABHA_KEEP_AWAKE_BATTERY=open
# Permission relay (SECURITY.md layer 4, docs/inbox-protocol.md section 9): a worker's permission prompt goes to you
# through the relay. SABHA_RELAY_WAIT: seconds the pane waits for your answer before its own prompt shows (default
# 1500 = 25 min; capped at 1780, under the hook's 1800 s timeout). `bin/sabha-perm snippet` prints the Claude Code
# settings to paste into your own settings file (sabha never edits one): defaultMode from SABHA_PERMISSION_MODE
# (default, acceptEdits, plan, ...) and the allow rules in SABHA_PERMISSION_ALLOW, separated by ";". The word
# "default" in the list stands for the shipped rules (reads in SABHA_IMPL, SABHA_WS and sabha; writes in SABHA_IMPL's
# inbox/, status/, questions/, for-me.items and SABHA_WS's worktrees, never their git hooks or .claude/; a few
# read-only commands; sabha-perm), so
# "default;Bash(make test)" adds one rule and a list without it replaces them. A test or build rule lets every role
# run code it wrote itself with no prompt (SECURITY.md layer 4). The snippet's deny part is always printed.
#SABHA_RELAY_WAIT=1500
#SABHA_PERMISSION_MODE=default
#SABHA_PERMISSION_ALLOW="default"
# Upstream move watcher (docs/upstream-watch.md): `sabha upstream` reads, once a day when sabha-watch runs it, whether a
# repo in your repos list (columns repo, dir, base-ref) was archived, renamed, lost its base branch, changed layout or had
# its upstream merged in, through the read-only bin/sabha-gh. A new finding is a for-me.items entry and one brief to
# SABHA_UPSTREAM_TO. Probes are paths tracked on the base branch; a pair is repo:base:upstream. Set SABHA_GH_USER too.
#SABHA_UPSTREAM_CONF=/path/to/repos.conf
#SABHA_UPSTREAM_ORG=your-org
#SABHA_UPSTREAM_PROBE_myrepo="apps/ docs/"
#SABHA_UPSTREAM_PAIRS="myrepo:release/next:dev"
#SABHA_UPSTREAM_TO=Dispatcher
SABHA_WAITING="Reviewer1 Reviewer2"
# Optional: "Name=task text;Name=task text" for a role whose status file has no Current: or Idle: line.
#SABHA_TASK_FALLBACK="Name=task text"

# GitHub reads (docs/github-layer.md). SABHA_GH_USER: the login gh must be on, so a wrong account stops with one line;
# SABHA_GH_TTL: cache seconds (600); SABHA_GH_MIN_LEFT: stop below this much API quota (200). `sabha prs` searches
# SABHA_PRS_ORGS and SABHA_PRS_REPOS (space separated) for the open PRs of SABHA_PRS_AUTHOR.
#SABHA_GH_USER=your-github-login
#SABHA_PRS_AUTHOR=your-github-login
#SABHA_PRS_ORGS="your-org"
#SABHA_PRS_REPOS="you/your-repo"
# `sabha shipped owner/repo#n`: the branches a change is promoted through, in order, one key per repo (the part after
# the slash, other characters as _). SABHA_GH_USER, SABHA_GH_TTL and SABHA_GH_MIN_LEFT are described in docs/github-layer.md.
#SABHA_CHAIN_your_repo="dev staging main"
# sabha decide: where its three files go (a relative value is read from SABHA_IMPL; each can also be given on the
# command line). Without SABHA_DECIDE_QUESTIONS each answer goes to questions/item-<id>.md.
#SABHA_DECIDE_OUT=$SABHA_IMPL/board-updates
#SABHA_DECIDE_QUESTIONS=$SABHA_IMPL/questions/my-pr-questions.md
#SABHA_DECISION_LOG=$SABHA_IMPL/decisions.log
# The progress board is the default view: the URL of your published board. /sabha-board points you to it first, then
# walks FOR-ME.md. Empty = no board yet (sabha doctor warns); FOR-ME.md stays the offline fallback.
SABHA_BOARD_URL=""
# sabha fill: all optional; read from the environment first, else from here (the environment wins, as for SABHA_STOP). Percent points kept free below the stop line, the load5 above which heavy briefs wait, the cost in
# points of a size S, M or L brief, and the words that mark a brief heavy when it has no "heavy: yes|no" header line.
#SABHA_FILL_RESERVE=10
#SABHA_FILL_LOAD5_MAX=20
#SABHA_FILL_COST_S=2
#SABHA_FILL_COST_M=4
#SABHA_FILL_COST_L=8
#SABHA_FILL_HEAVY_WORDS="gradle jest docker playwright e2e pnpm"

# Example question item for for-me.items (blocks are separated by a line that is exactly ---). Keys: ask, q, explain,
# opta..opth ("option text | consequence | example"), rec. Plain items need none of them.
#---
#id: 99
#utc: 2026-10-14T09:00:00Z
#owner: Relay
#title: Which branch gets the demo
#ask: Pat
#q: Which branch should the demo be built from?
#explain: The demo freezes whatever the branch holds, so a late change would not show.
#opta: develop | newest code, may be unstable | includes yesterday's unreviewed merge
#optb: release/1.2 | stable, a day old | what Alex already reviewed
#rec: b

# Chain view (docs/chain-view.md): the repos whose "NAME #N" and "NAME#N" mentions in board rows count as PRs (space
# separated OWNER/NAME); OWNER/NAME#N is always read.
#SABHA_CHAIN_REPOS="you/your-repo you/other-repo"
# sabha intake: the folder (default <SABHA_IMPL>/intake, with drop/ for notes), the inbox that gets one brief per ask, and
# the sender of the briefs and owner of the board rows.
#SABHA_INTAKE_DIR=$SABHA_IMPL/intake
#SABHA_INTAKE_TO=Dispatcher
#SABHA_INTAKE_FROM=intake
